detail.abstract:
Brand equity represents approximately 30-35% of enterprise value across public markets, yet it remains outside formal governance frameworks, risk classification systems, and regulatory disclosure requirements. This paper introduces Brand Asset Risk Governance (BARG) as a structured, empirically validated discipline that treats brand as a measurable, monitorable, and governable financial asset. We present the BARG Index (BARGI)-a multi-component scoring methodology linking governance maturity, narrative signal acceleration, stakeholder amplification, and digital risk exposure to market outcomes including collapse probability, crisis containment duration, and financial recovery trajectory.&nbsp;
<div>
 Using a cross-validated dataset of 200 named public company incidents spanning 12 sectors and 30 years of documented brand-related governance failures, we demonstrate that the BARGI collapse prediction model achieves a cross-validated ROC AUC of 0.8979-exceeding the predictive accuracy of most credit risk models currently deployed across financial services. Cases scored in the Critical band (BARGI ≥ 75) converted to observed collapse or severe decline at an 82.1% rate. The Severe Decline prediction model achieved an AUC of 0.8300.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 An independent anchor dataset of 10 high-profile cases-including Silicon Valley Bank, Credit Suisse, Meta Platforms, BP, and Boeing-documents aggregate modeled governance-attributed losses exceeding $300 billion, sourced to public market data and verified through Reuters reporting.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 We propose BARG as the foundational governance standard for brand risk across capital markets, insurance underwriting, regulatory disclosure, and corporate governance. The framework's four governance domains-Visibility, Response, Governance, and Dependency-map directly to the four NABARG institutional principles of Observability, Measurability, Governability, and Predictability, establishing a complete institutional architecture for a previously ungoverned category of financial risk.
</div>
